bin/ - IC generation and editing tools
=====================================

Add this directory to your PATH and every tool below is callable from
anywhere; each one resolves the repository location from its own path, so no
environment setup is needed.

    export PATH=$PWD/bin:$PATH

Every tool takes --help.

IC editing (setupfiles/)
------------------------

  setbfield     Apply a magnetic field to a tipsy IC. A field is
                profile x strength x direction x kind, so configurations are
                combinations rather than menu entries. --kind potential builds
                a vector potential A and reads B = curl A with a chosen
                glassgen flavour; --clean then removes div B with the SAME
                operator family, so the IC is clean in the measure it was
                built in.
                  -> setupfiles/IC_setBField.py

  setquantity   Edit quantities: chainable operations over particles AND aux
                in one pass, with one --where selection vocabulary shared by
                all of them.
                  ops: set velocity center remove keep rotate permute time
                       bfield spin add
                  -> setupfiles/IC_setQuantity.py

  setimpact     Two-body impact / merger from one or two snapshots (one =
                clone). Masses measured from the particles, centre-of-mass
                frame, keplerian or simple orbit parameterisation.
                  -> setupfiles/IC_setup_impact.py

  divbclean     Statically clean div B on an existing IC (particles frozen,
                only B and psi evolve). --flavour must match whatever the
                field was built with; setbfield --clean runs this same code
                with the flavour already matched.
                  -> icgenerator/glassgen_python/mhd_divBclean.py

Export / interpolation (setupfiles/)
------------------------------------

  tipsyexport   Convert a tipsy IC into another code's IC format. One entry
                point over the seven ICconvert_tipsyto*.py converters:
                  arepo athena gizmo opengadget3 phantom ramses sphexa
                Flags after --format pass straight through, and
                `--format FMT --help` shows that converter's own options.
                  tipsyexport --list-formats
                  -> setupfiles/ICconvert_tipsyto<fmt>.py

  interpolatetogrid
                Resample an SPH snapshot onto a structured target - uniform
                grid, AMR octree, Voronoi mesh or projection map - and export
                it (npz | hdf5 | ramses | cell-table | native). Use this when
                you want a MESH of the fields; use tipsyexport when you want a
                runnable IC for a specific code.
                  -> setupfiles/IC_interpolate_to_grid.py

  tipsyimport   The reverse: convert another code's finished RUN back into
                tipsy snapshots for the analysis pipeline. Eight formats:
                  arepo athena gizmo opengadget3 phantom pkdgrav3 ramses sphexa
                Each takes <input> <output> <idx_interval> [paramfile]; the
                paramfile is REQUIRED for athena and ramses.
                  tipsyimport --list-formats
                  -> setupfiles/ICconvert_<fmt>totipsy.py

Not wrapped: POLARIS export - IC_export_polaris.py takes a galaxy CUTOUT
(.npz from _polaris_locate_galaxy.py) and --out-dir, not a tipsy snapshot, so
it does not share this shape. Run it directly.

Glass generation and resampling (icgenerator/glassgen_python/)
--------------------------------------------------------------

  glassrelax    Relax a particle distribution toward a target density.
  glassresample Resample a snapshot: --op split | merge | reconstruct.
                This is where split/merge live - setquantity does NOT do them.
  glasstable    Build/inspect the density table the reconstruction uses.

These three are thin forwarders to the scripts of the same name in
icgenerator/glassgen_python/, which resolve their package root from their own
location; a symlink or a move would break that, so the forwarder exec's them
in place.

Typical chain
-------------

    setquantity  ic.00000 -o cen.00000 center --on potential-min
    glassresample cen.00000 --op split --ntimes 2 -o fine
    setbfield    fine.00000 -o fine_B.00000 \
                 --profile rho --direction azi --strength beta-th --value 50 \
                 --kind potential --flavour gdforce --clean --target-mean 1e-3
    setimpact    fine_B.00000 -o merger.00000 \
                 --orbit keplerian --separation 217 --pericentre 20 --ecc 1.0
    interpolatetogrid merger.00000 mesh.h5 --grid amr \
                 --fields rho,vx,vy,vz --output-format ramses
    tipsyexport  merger.00000 merger_gizmo.hdf5 --format gizmo --mhd

...and after that run finishes, bring it back for analysis:

    tipsyimport  gizmo_rundir run_giz 10 run.param --format gizmo

Note tipsyimport re-centres the particles into the gasoline [-L/2, L/2] box
(per-axis, on the .param periods; on the data centroid when no .param is
given), so a round trip differs from the original by a rigid translation
unless the input was already box-centred. That is deliberate - the analysis
framework expects centred snapshots.

(divbclean is not in that chain because setbfield --clean already ran it; use
it standalone on an IC whose field came from somewhere else.)

Notes
-----

  * setbfield/setquantity/setimpact write a JSON audit beside the output
    (OUT_setBField.json, OUT_impact.json) recording the configuration and the
    resulting diagnostics.
  * Aux files are carried through every operation: removing particles drops
    their aux rows, and rotations turn the packed vector aux together with its
    scalar component twins (BFieldx/y/z, wx/wy/wz).
  * mhd_divBclean.py (icgenerator/glassgen_python/) cleans div B on an
    existing IC as a standalone pass; setbfield --clean calls the same code.
